          "derivation": "كُحْل kuḥl (vulgar kuḥul/koḥl) 'finely ground stibnite used on the eyelids' is the ordinary noun of the root k-ḥ-l (verb كَحَلَ kaḥala 'to apply kohl'); كَحَّال kaḥḥāl is the regular faʿʿāl intensive/professional noun, 'kohl-applier', which in medieval Islamic medicine is the standard word for an oculist (the treatises of ʿAlī ibn ʿĪsā al-Kaḥḥāl of Baghdad, d. c.1010, are the classic example) — both are confirmed. The third item is only half right. The form is real: beside kuḥl, Andalusi Arabic had كُحُول kuḥūl, and the Latin alchemists took the phrase with the article as al-kohol; Klein p.46 records 'al-kohl, vulgar pronunciation of al-kuhl'. But the gloss 'highly purified spirits' is a European, not an Arabic, meaning: the word denoted a finely triturated powder, then any sublimate or essence, and only in 16th–18th c. European alchemy did 'alcohol of wine' come to mean spirit of wine. Pihan (Dict. étym. des mots… dérivés de l'arabe, p.20) says so flatly: 'le nom de cette dernière liqueur n'a pour équivalent en arabe que rūḥ al-ʿaraqī, traduction littérale du français'. So the chart antedates a European sense into Arabic.",
