          "derivation": "کُحل and کَحّال are straightforward Arabic loans in Persian, taken over with the Greco-Arabic medical tradition; Mo'in tags both [ع.] (= Arabic): 'کحال [ع.] (ص. اِ.) کسی که کحل (سرمه) به چشم اشخاص می‌کشد؛ سرمه‌کش … در قدیم کحال به کسی گفته می‌شد که هم سرمه به چشم کسان می‌کشید و هم جراحات و امراض چشم را علاج می‌کرد … طبیبی که دردهای چشم را درمان کند؛ چشم‌پزشک' and 'کحل [ع.] (اِ.) سنگ سرمه. سرمه. هر چه در چشم کشند برای شفای چشم'; the Vahshi couplet quoted in the box uses کحل in exactly that sense. These two are confirmed. کُحول 'alcohol' is different in kind: it is not an old Arabic loan into Persian but a modern scientific term — Arabic كُحول kuḥūl was pressed into service in the 19th–20th c. as the chemical name for alcohol, on the model of the European word that had itself come from al-kuḥl, and Persian took it in that technical register (the everyday Persian word remains الکل, node #5). Mo'in's two-volume abridgement has مکحول but no headword کحول, so the term could not be confirmed lexicographically in Persian; hence 'plausible' rather than 'confirmed'.",
